A||||9||JohnnyK||Slave cylinder||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||12-28-2000||05:13 PM||||Well, I'm pretty sure the master isn't leaking, and I replaced the line (albeit it is custom made with couplers, which I don't trust), and the actual clutch disc, rb and pressure plate are new, but it still doesn't shift right (loses fluid slowly). Anyone have a slave for sale cheap? Can I just buy a rebuild kit? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||2xsess||12-28-2000||05:45 PM||||Do a search, there is a bunch of threads on this subject.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||12-28-2000||06:30 PM||||Yeah, I started most of them... Still nothing on my subject specifically.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||12-29-2000||02:02 PM||||Rebuild kit was around $40 last August, and a new aftermarket one cost $63.  I went with the new one, and so far none of the problems mentioned about heat soak affecting the aftermarket ones (cast iron) have plagued me.  I've heard of new GM units going for less, but my dealer wanted around $185.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||12-29-2000||06:31 PM||||How can I tell if that is my problem? Where will it be leaking?||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||12-29-2000||11:05 PM||||When the master leaks, it is usually on the inside of the car, so your carpet will be wet with brake fluid.  Unmistakeable smell.  Otherwise check your connections.  Rebuild kit was cheap for the master cylinder, if I remember correctly, but it's been a few years since I did that.  I blled mine with a Mity-Vac, but others here have had good luck with gravity bleeding. Try this link for some good info, if you haven't already found it. <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||12-30-2000||01:14 PM||||But how can I tell if the slave is leaking? WHere abouts does it leak? Past the plunger? (Damn this snowy weather, I can't really check out whats happening on my car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||12-30-2000||05:23 PM||||I went through the clutch problem last year with my car. Someone said that maybe the fluid was leaking past the seal in the master cylinder from the side that was pressurizing the line to the slave cylinder to the opposite side as you held the clutch floored, the slave would slowly creep back to the retracted position. I did several things to fix my clutch problem but I think they were right. I replaced my MC with a remanufactured one from parts america ($109.00) and the slave no longer retracted while I held the pedal to the floor.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||12-30-2000||10:59 PM||||If you want to look for a leak in the engine compartment, I'd invest in some Simple Green or Castrol Purple Cleaner and get it spotless first, then look for where it gets dirty the quickest.  Brake fluid at least as the distinct pine smell, so it won't be as easlily confused with oil or coolant.  If the slave is the leaker, it will go past the piston seal into the boot area, which you can check if you are careful.  My bleeder screw was so rusty I couldn't get it to seal, and you can't buy a just bleeder screw for these, so it was a rebuild kit or a new slave for me.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero Fanatic||12-31-2000||10:05 AM||||About a year ago I experience the same problem? New clutch installed, but impossible to shift. The mechanics at the shop could barely even bleed the system enough to drive home. I found what the problem was myself. Each time I depressed the clutch pedal the clutch master push-rod attached to the pedal was causing the plunger in the clutch master cylinder to retract too far out the end of the clutch master cylinder therefor allowing air into the system each time I engaged the clutch. I could see this for myself by getting under the dash and removing the clutch master cylinder dust cover. I solved this problem by taking a lock washer about the size of a dime, pulling in apart, and then carefully install it around your push-rod. Make sure your lock washer is a little smaller than the retaining washer, also make sure it can easily slide up and down on the push-rod. Then circlip the push-rod back into the clutch master cylinder. Re-install the dust cover. Re-install clutch master cylinder. Re-attach push-rod to clutch pedal. Fill with Dot 3 brake fluid. Bleed. Now hopefully your shifting nice and smooth  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">! Click for diagram                            <A HREF="http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.gif" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.gif</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 12-31-2000).]||||reg||1
